 "vyzhimka": {
  "tekst": "For a small LA service business where customers keep pushing for discounts, you’re probably looking for help with pricing strategy and sales/objection handling—not generic marketing.\n\nA few LA options worth considering:\n\n- LA Regional SBDC: probably my first stop. They provide no-cost, confidential one-on-one advising to small businesses in Los Angeles and surrounding counties. Ask specifically for someone experienced in service-business pricing, margins, sales process, and customer objections. ([smallbizla.org](https://smallbizla.org/?utm_source=openai))\n- SCORE Los Angeles: also free one-on-one mentoring. Their mentor directory specifically lets you filter for both “Pricing” and “Sales,” as well as your industry. ([score.org](https://www.score.org/losangeles/mentors/?utm_source=openai))\n- LA BusinessSource Centers: if you're within the City of Los Angeles, these centers offer one-on-one small-business consulting and other business-development assistance. ([cid.lacity.gov](https://cid.lacity.gov/business-services/businesssource-centers?utm_source=openai))\n- Private sales coach: if you want more hands-on work—rewriting your estimate/pitch, role-playing “that's too expensive,” improving closing, etc.—a sales coach specializing in service businesses can make more sense. For example, Outcomes Not Ideas explicitly works on value-based pricing and objection handling for service businesses. ([outcomesnotideas.com](https://www.outcomesnotideas.com/?utm_source=openai))\n\nBefore paying a consultant thousands, I’d book an SBDC and SCORE session and bring your actual numbers: what you charge, your costs/margin, 5–10 recent quotes, how many converted, and examples of what customers say when they haggle.\n\nIf you tell me what kind of service business you run (e.g. cleaning, landscaping, photography, contracting, auto, professional services) and roughly what customers typically spend ($200 vs. $2,000 vs. $20,000), I can narrow this to 2–3 LA people/organizations that fit your situation.",
